Oh...hmmm.. I always thought that "korka" just meant "cork", (i.e. Quercus
suber, or the like). I didn't even realize it was bark in general (which I
would have assumed was ricpi'a)  So, yes, to your suggestion, despite it
not being official.

  As for a cork stopper, kokyvelbo'i or punvelbo'i (more general for
synthetic stoppers)
            --gejyspa



On Fri, Jul 26, 2013 at 12:34 PM, Pierre Abbat <phma@bezitopo.org> wrote:

> x1 is a quantity of/contains/is made of cork/bark from tree/species x2
> The English, Spanish, and Russian source words all mean a stopper or bark
> suitable for making stoppers; neither refers to bark in general, though
> Latin
> "cortex", which is at least partly the source of "cork" and "corcho", can
> mean
> bark in general or cork specifically. ("cork" may come partly from
> "quercus",
> as corks are made from oak bark.)
>
> *Should "korka" be restricted to bark suitable for making corks?
> *How about "ricpi'a" for bark in general?
> *What do we call a stopper, which may or may not be made of cork?
>
> Pierre
